      "hypothesis": "Stats kernel register-level reduction, insufficient parallelism",
      "evidence": "Attempt 4 repaired the above by using only one program per row, accumulating all partials in registers and writing out a single mean/inv per row. This is correct and matches the output contract, but achieves effectively identical performance to the incumbent (median_ms within 1%). Thus, register-level reduction alone does not resolve a major bottleneck for this problem size.",
      "certainty": "High"
    },
    {
      "hypothesis": "Type errors in loop-carried variables",
      "evidence": "Attempt 2 failed at compilation due to a type mismatch in the reduction loop (mixing int32/uint32). Attempt 3 failed at the proposal stage for using unsupported tl.uint32 scalar. These are blocking bugs but not performance bottlenecks; once fixed, they do not hinder correct output.",
      "certainty": "High"
    },
    {
      "hypothesis": "Apply kernel bottleneck",
      "evidence": "The apply kernel is unchanged in all attempts and is not identified as a bottleneck by profile or results. The stats kernel is the only target of optimization so far.",
